# Catalog Noema Rules

## 1. Source of truth

Before changing behavior, align with:

- `docs/reference/product-contract.md`
- `docs/reference/data-model.md`
- `docs/README.md`

If the requested change conflicts with them, do not silently implement it.

## 2. Product scope

Do not add unless explicitly approved:

- online payments;
- customer accounts;
- order persistence in DB;
- CRM, marketplace, warehouse logic;
- delivery as backend workflow;
- microservices or custom backend outside current stack.

Cart and order remain client-side in V1.

## 3. Architecture boundaries

- `app/` routes, pages, metadata, server actions, route handlers
- `components/` UI and interaction wiring
- `store/` client-side state only
- `lib/` domain logic, mapping, infra helpers
- `types/` domain contracts
- `migrations/` schema and RLS

Do not put business rules into UI.
Do not duplicate domain logic across layers.

## 4. Multi-tenant safety

This is a multi-tenant project by business and subdomain.

Never allow one business to read or mutate another business's data.
Never trust `businessId`, `productId`, `categoryId`, `brandId`, or `slug` from the client without server verification.
Always verify access on the server through `business_user` or equivalent ownership logic.

Be careful when changing:

- `middleware.ts`
- `lib/host.ts`
- `app/[slug]/page.tsx`
- `app/admin/*/actions.ts`

## 5. Supabase rules

Use the correct client:

- `lib/supabase.ts` for public/anon reads and client auth flows
- `lib/supabase-server.ts` for authenticated server-side work
- `lib/supabase-admin.ts` only when bypassing RLS is truly necessary

Do not add new client-side writes to core tables like:

- `business`
- `category`
- `product`
- `brand`
- `business_user`

All access-sensitive mutations should go through server-side code.

## 6. Client vs server

Default to server components.
Use `"use client"` only when browser interaction is actually needed.

Client components must not contain database permission logic.
Client components must not be the source of truth for business rules.

## 7. Data boundary

Database rows are snake_case.
App domain types are camelCase.

Map data at the boundary.
Do not spread raw Supabase row shapes deep into UI code.

Reuse domain types from `types/index.ts`.
Do not create parallel type systems for the same entities.

## 8. Server actions

In server actions:

- validate input early;
- resolve user early;
- verify business access early;
- keep permission checks explicit;
- revalidate only needed paths.

If touching large files like:

- `app/admin/product/actions.ts`
- `app/admin/business/actions.ts`
- `components/business/business-profile-editor-sheet.tsx`

prefer extracting helpers instead of growing them further.

## 9. Public catalog invariants

Core files:

- `app/[slug]/page.tsx`
- `components/catalog/*`
- `components/cart/*`
- `store/cart.ts`
- `store/catalog-filters.ts`
- `lib/order.ts`
- `lib/promo.ts`

Preserve:

- mobile-first catalog behavior;
- client-side cart and order flow;
- deterministic search/filter/cart logic;
- per-business promo isolation.

Do not introduce server order processing unless explicitly requested.

## 10. Temporary code

Do not add new debug/admin/temp routes to production routing unless explicitly requested.
Do not leave temporary internal tools exposed by URL.

## 11. State management

Use Zustand as the global client state layer.
Do not introduce Redux or another state library.
Keep per-business state isolated by `businessId`.

## 12. Change discipline

Prefer small, explicit changes.
Do not do broad rewrites when a narrow fix is enough.
Do not add abstractions for future use.
If a change affects product scope, data model, or architecture expectations, update docs.

## 13. Non-negotiable principle

Every change must be:

- product-aligned;
- tenant-safe;
- security-aware;
- simple enough to maintain through AI-assisted development.



